88 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (The Railway Reservation System is a web-based application that allows a user to view available trains, book a ticket, cancel a ticket and check the ticket status using a PNR number. It is a beginner-level Data Structures and Algorithms \(DSA\) project built with Java and Spring Boot for the backend and HTML, CSS and JavaScript for the frontend.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
89 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (An Array stores the seats of every train, a Queue stores the waiting list of passengers in First-In-First-Out \(FIFO\) order, and a HashMap gives quick access to trains by train number and to tickets by PNR number. When a confirmed ticket is cancelled, the first passenger of the waiting Queue is automatically moved to the freed seat. Java string operations are used to trim and validate the passenger name and other text entered by the user.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
90 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (The browser sends the booking, cancellation and status requests to Spring Boot REST endpoints, which return the result as JSON, and the page displays the ticket details. The project uses a small predefined list of trains and does not need a database, payment or any advanced features, which keeps the scope suitable for a college-level submission.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 57 0 R
/T (Introduction)
/E (Introduction)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
92 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (Railway reservation is one of the most common real-life examples of seat allotment. A train has a fixed number of seats; when all seats are booked, new passengers are placed on a waiting list, and when someone cancels, the waiting passengers are confirmed in the order in which they booked. This behaviour maps naturally to Arrays, Queues and HashMaps.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
93 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 57 0 R
/E (This project demonstrates the idea on a small scale. The user selects a train and enters the passenger name and age. The browser \(frontend\) sends the request to the Java backend using a REST request. The backend validates the input, allots a seat if one is free or places the passenger in the waiting Queue, generates a PNR number and sends the ticket details back as JSON. The user can later cancel the ticket or check its status using the PNR.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
